WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.830
My name is Brent Dicesare.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.870
I'm one half of WD Detailing.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.370
And today we're going to be cleaning

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:22.370
a 1978 Sterling Nova that
sat in a barn since 1985.

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:34.140
So, a Sterling Nova is a kit car

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.830
which is on a Volkswagen
Beetle chassis and engine.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.710
And it's really unique
because of the way it looks

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.470
and the way that the top opens.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.390
There's no doors on this car.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.710
The way that you get in and out

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.370
is literally by the whole top lifting up.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.690
We had to take the top off of this car

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.180
because it actually couldn't
hold its own weight up

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.780
and we didn't want to risk
it falling on one of us.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.480
As soon as we took the top off of the car,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.770
we found this mouse nest right
on the backside of the dash.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.040
It was made out of insulation or carpet

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.780
or parts from the seats.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.380
Any random fibers that
they can find in the car

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.360
they'll take and make a nest out of.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.143
And then we sanitized the area afterwards

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:20.159
with TriClean, which is a cleaner

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:22.170
that will kill all the bacteria.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.144
Cars attract mice because

00:01:24.144 --> 00:01:26.169
they know that it's a dark space,

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:28.050
and when these cars sit in a barn

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.280
and nobody's touching them
for 30-plus, 40-plus years,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.480
they're not going to be bothered,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.850
so they're going to
take advantage of that.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.780
And then after that

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.040
we were able to take the seats out

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.170
and really get in there
and clean everything.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.695
We found that these carpets

00:01:41.695 --> 00:01:43.530
were completely disintegrated,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.450
and they're definitely going
to have to replace them.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.075
And we actually haven't really seen

00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:49.416
carpets this bad before.

00:01:49.416 --> 00:01:51.557
So if I had to make an assumption

00:01:51.557 --> 00:01:53.382
on why this happened,

00:01:53.382 --> 00:01:56.880
it's a combination of
them sitting over time

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.210
and the mice getting into
them and eating them up.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.730
And under the carpet, as
soon as we pulled it out,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.821
I looked down and found a nice surprise

00:02:05.821 --> 00:02:07.562
of a dead mouse.

00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:09.660
We disposed of it off camera,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.240
but we just had to pick
it up with our gloves

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.000
and toss it in the garbage can.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.065
There's no nice way to
throw away a dead mouse.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.000
We started vacuuming the floor pans,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.742
and then we sanitized them
again with our TriClean.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.400
And then we used our steamer behind it

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.173
to kill anything else.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.360
And then we wiped it all
away with microfiber towels.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.000
So, this dashboard was
covered in dust completely.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.830
And at this point we're
still using TriClean

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:56.970
to clean the interior,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.920
because we want to disinfect everything.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.140
We knew that this car
isn't going to be started

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.100
for quite a while, because it doesn't run

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.860
and it needs a lot of repair work,

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.200
so if cleaner gets inside the buttons

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.090
or inside of the switches,

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.830
it's not going to be the end of the world,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.690
because it'll definitely dry up before

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.515
there's any kind of electricity
running through this car.

00:03:29.013 --> 00:03:31.867
So, if you want to get a barn find,

00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:35.130
a good way is searching
on Facebook Marketplace.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.330
The people that we work with

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.280
specifically search for these cars,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.480
and then they bring them to us

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.610
because they sat for so long.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.327
A lot of it's word of mouth.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.777
So, for these seats, they were covered in

00:03:54.777 --> 00:03:57.300
anything that the mice could put on them.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.090
So the first thing that
we do is vacuum them,

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.800
and then we'll go ahead and extract them.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.870
So we use our extractor soap,

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.690
and then we'll use our drill
brush to agitate the soap

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.000
and really work it into those fibers.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.440
And then we'll use our extractor,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.330
which shoots hot water into the fibers

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.130
and also sucks it away at the same time.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.261
So it's a vacuum, but
it's a vacuum with water.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.040
The exterior of this
car was covered in dust

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.600
from front to back,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.717
and then there was some wood chips

00:04:29.717 --> 00:04:32.790
and other stuff in the headlight crevice,

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.280
which, I'm pretty sure on this kit,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.170
there's supposed to be a headlight cover,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.895
but for some reason they were missing

00:04:38.895 --> 00:04:40.253
on this specific car.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.720
And here we're vacuuming
out some of the jambs,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.420
which we like to do before
we pressure wash off the car,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.400
because if there's any
wood chips or anything,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.250
it might scratch the paint.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.320
So, on the wheels,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.410
they were covered with some
sort of green substance,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.780
which, I don't know if it was mold or not.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.580
And to clean this tire, we'll go ahead

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.280
and pressure wash
everything off of it first.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.930
And then we'll use our P&amp;S Brake Buster

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.150
on the tire and the rim,

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.010
and then we'll use an
assortment of brushes,

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.800
usually soft-bristle
brushes and a tire brush.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.884
And then we'll pressure wash it all away.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.730
The paint on this car was
in pretty good condition.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.460
The only thing that we
noticed was deteriorating

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.710
was the black paint.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:41.940
So, what we're spraying on this car

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.890
is a paint-safe degreaser

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.630
so we can loosen up all that dirt

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.640
before we pressure wash it away.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.020
And what that's going to do

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.545
is minimize any kind of
swirl marks or scratches.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.330
Paint chips can be damaging to a car,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.720
only because it can cause rust to form

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.570
if there's metal underneath
where the paint was.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.850
But there was no rust on this body at all

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.803
because it's made out of fiberglass.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.090
So, we're starting to foam the car here

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.250
using our pressure washer and foam cannon.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.242
And this is the part that a lot of people

00:06:19.242 --> 00:06:22.023
find the most satisfying
in the whole process.

00:06:22.023 --> 00:06:24.420
And there's two reasons why
people use foam cannons.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.240
The first one is as a presoak,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.250
so they can loosen up all the dirt

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:30.540
that's on the surface of the car.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.810
But also, instead of just
putting soap in a bucket,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.240
it helps you put soap over the entire car,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.083
which makes the process safer
for the paint and easier.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.060
And after foaming the car,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.790
we'll use microfiber
mitts on the entire car

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.173
to wash it down.

00:07:07.403 --> 00:07:09.870
And now we're using our Rupes buffer

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.597
to take any of the oxidation off

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.440
the top layer of the paint,

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.980
which will make it a
lot shinier, less dull,

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.800
and give it that overall
wow factor at the end.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.630
So this is just, like, a paint correction.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:22.830
And when it came to the black paint,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.300
we didn't even polish it at all,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.030
because it was starting
to come off of the car

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.898
and we didn't want to ruin or take away

00:07:29.898 --> 00:07:32.490
any of that paint that was already there.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.100
There wasn't too much that
we could personally do.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.440
It's something that would
have to either be redone

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.153
or just left as is.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:41.760
And after the whole car is cleaned

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.560
and we're ready to do
the finishing touches,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.420
we'll do a tire shine.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.670
And as that dries, it leaves the tires

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.571
looking nice and matte black,

00:07:49.571 --> 00:07:52.290
and it just gives it
that overall wow factor

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.850
for the customer.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.620
And the absolute last thing we'll do

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.560
is spray a ceramic sealant
over the entire car.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.110
It'll protect the paint just a little bit,

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.330
but it'll also give it a nice shine

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.330
and just complete the detail.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.371
And that's it.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.540
I was super happy with the
way that this car turned out.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.180
I actually personally
want to buy it myself.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.520
We'll see how that goes
in the near future,

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.953
when it's done being fixed up.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.500
When it comes to cleaning barn finds,

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.240
if I can give any advice,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.906
it's just to take your time

00:08:25.906 --> 00:08:27.810
and really do your research.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.718
That way, you don't make any

00:08:29.718 --> 00:08:31.609
super-detrimental mistakes.

00:08:31.609 --> 00:08:33.390
Because the last thing you want to do

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.580
is find something that has original paint

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.690
or an original interior and damage it.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.266
But also, have fun with the process.

